On Sun, Sep 17, 2017 at 4:40 PM, Michael Turniansky <mturniansky@gmail.com>
wrote:

>   It is not.  The . is an indicator of a glottal stop or pause.  Said
> pause is mandatory in speech (before a word starting with a vowel, after a
> "la", "lai" or "doi", and after any word ending with a consonant), and the
> dot is there to remind you of it.  It also provides a nice visual break in
> long blocks of text, but if you are putting spaces in between words, you
> don't need it.  For example, the utterance you wrote can also be written
> pamlatu cupinxe.ilemlatu cutaske and the parser would still understand it.
>
> On Sat, Sep 16, 2017 at 10:08 PM, Forrest Cahoon <forrest.cahoon@gmail.com
> > wrote:
>
>> I'm just starting to learn Lojban, using the Crash Course. I came across
>> this:
>>
>> *pa mlatu cu pinxe i le mlatu cu taske*
>> *There is a cat that is drinking. The cat is thirsty.*
>>
>> The course has introduced *.i* as a sentence separator, but not a lone
>> *i* without the leading dot. When I tried this on the lojban parser at
>> https://lojban.github.io/ilmentufa/glosser/glosser.htm, it appeared to
>> be accepted as valid Lojban, so I guess it's not a mistake.
>>
>> What are the rules here? Is the dot not always required in front of the i
>> sentence separator?
>>
>> Forrest
